WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.840
I think most of us have seen games like this: it’s&nbsp;
turn five or so, and a player is getting ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.560
go to attacks. Immediately, a chorus erupts from&nbsp;
the would-be attacker’s opponents. “C’mon man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.440
I don’t have anything!” “Hey don’t attack&nbsp;
me, I missed a land drop.” “Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.720
you’re gonna attack me, my deck is just trying&nbsp;
to be funny.” I like to call this style of table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:28.040
engagement SBS—“Smol Bean Syndrome”—when a player&nbsp;
goes out of their way to paint their deck as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:33.760
in a bad spot and deserving of nothing but mercy.&nbsp;
Now, commander’s nature as a multiplayer format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.640
means that table politics naturally play a&nbsp;
major role in a lot of games. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.560
a lot of people play EDH specifically for a less&nbsp;
cutthroat MTG experience—not everybody at every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.440
table is trying to win quickly, or even win at&nbsp;
all. I’m not a big fan of most forms of Smol Bean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.120
Syndrome due to their tendency to discourage&nbsp;
attacks and artificially lengthen games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.920
but I can accept that not everybody is&nbsp;
trying to play the same kind of EDH I am.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.160
However, there are also people who share my&nbsp;
desire to play quicker and more cutthroat games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:08.200
and who employ Smol Bean Syndrome more&nbsp;
tactically. This may be intentional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.080
but it also might not be, which brings me&nbsp;
to a major reason for SBS’s existence—deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.440
inconsistency. If somebody has an&nbsp;
inconsistent deck, one with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.640
powerful and/or explosive cards but a lack of&nbsp;
support or connective tissue for such cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.520
there will naturally be moments of popping off and&nbsp;
moments of the opposite of popping off. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.640
that such a player can be telling the truth&nbsp;
when they say they’re having a whiff moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.360
but this can still be a deception in some sense&nbsp;
of the word. Let’s try an exercise real quick.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.160
Here’s a graph of their deck’s performance,&nbsp;
with the Y-axis being power and the X-axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:49.000
being percentile of power at a given moment,&nbsp;
you know 20% of the time they’ll be at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.200
this level of powerful, 80% of the time they’ll&nbsp;
be at least this much lower quantity of powerful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.640
Make sense? No? Great. You’ve obviously got&nbsp;
this moderately small percentage of the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.360
where their deck is absolutely going ham,&nbsp;
doing whatever it is that powerful decks do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.440
but also this gulf back here where their deck is&nbsp;
farting around doing nothing. Certainly, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.000
having a bad time here. But this is often a&nbsp;
situation arising from deckbuilding choices—a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.720
that dedicates too much space to the synergistic&nbsp;
and the explosive will have some big turns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.120
occasionally massive wins, but also a lot of&nbsp;
moments where it withers to a single removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.080
spell, or just can’t quite put the pieces of the&nbsp;
puzzle together. These moments could be made less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.640
frequent by running more of that connective&nbsp;
tissue—more filtering, more small-scale draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.720
and more small creatures and low-cost interaction&nbsp;
cards to protect themself. If they’re ramping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.240
to big cards, they could run a larger proportion&nbsp;
of small-scale ramp spells in their deck so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.480
successfully ramp to where they want to be more&nbsp;
than 40% of the time. When a person is piloting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.720
sort of all-or-nothing deck, Smol Bean Syndrome is&nbsp;
a form of power-level smoothing, a way of papering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.760
over the power crater they made for themselves and&nbsp;
bolstering these weakest moments. And I don’t mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:02.480
this as an attack necessarily—if this sounds like&nbsp;
you, please, take this as deck building advice.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.520
A related concept to Smol Bean Syndrome is people&nbsp;
who don’t verbally talk down their game state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:13.400
but who do play in a way that conceals how&nbsp;
well they’re doing. Now, at a cEDH table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.440
this is the norm, and as it should be at that&nbsp;
power. You’re trying to win a cutthroat game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.160
magic against other people trying to win even more&nbsp;
cutthroatedly, and you have every right to bluff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.240
to downplay your game state, and to otherwise&nbsp;
engage in low-level deception. At a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.840
middling power level though, this might not&nbsp;
be the best way to go. If you’re playing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.400
deck with a clear wincon, people know what to&nbsp;
expect even if they’re a bit scared about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.200
but if you’re playing a deck whose wins&nbsp;
result from weird tech pieces interacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.720
to create unexpected avalanches of value&nbsp;
and boardstate—well, people might get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.240
bit apprehensive. When a deck is doing a&nbsp;
lot of things but it’s unclear how it wins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.560
people may be asking themselves the question&nbsp;
“oh god, is it that? Or is it that?”

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.520
Additionally, there are some strategic ways of&nbsp;
playing that might involve saving resources, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:05.960
trying to force other people to use interaction&nbsp;
first. You might say to yourself, “hmm that card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.680
is spooky, but if I bluff no removal somebody&nbsp;
else might deal with it and they’ll have less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.680
removal when I wanna do something spooky later.”&nbsp;
My friend Mr. Hans calls this “playing like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.280
nerd,” and though it’s decent from a strategic&nbsp;
standpoint, it’s the kind of slightly-ratty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.480
playstyle that people will eventually catch onto.&nbsp;
Whether or not you get punished for playing shady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.600
or playing nerdy will depend on the table, but&nbsp;
you should keep in mind that human beings have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:36.120
a natural tendency to fear the unknown, even if&nbsp;
being unknown might feel like the correct play.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.960
For an example of a deck that performs almost the&nbsp;
opposite way, let’s talk about my Glissa deck—I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.880
talk about it a lot, and I will do so again here.&nbsp;
That deck consists of ramp, draw spells, removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:52.640
and big creatures. No bursty wins, no surprises,&nbsp;
and no weird tech pieces or out of place synergy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.440
cards hogging space. There’s nothing spicy&nbsp;
with the commander either—she just adds draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.400
to improve consistency and is a good blocker to&nbsp;
help with survivability. The deck simply does its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.680
battlecruiser thing—people who’ve played against&nbsp;
the deck know that’s what it does, and if people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:11.880
ask me what it does that’s what I’ll tell them.&nbsp;
If I’m currently behind while playing that deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.840
well guess what, when I get back on my feet&nbsp;
you can expect more ramp, draw, big creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.320
and removal. Get ready. It’s a very consistent&nbsp;
deck, and it’s also what I’ll call a transparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.160
deck. By this I mean, you can always approximate&nbsp;
how well it’s doing by looking at the boardstate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.440
cards in hand, and land count of the&nbsp;
deck. If the deck has a big board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.120
well the threat is right there, no extreme&nbsp;
synergies or combos to worry about later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.280
If the deck has a lot of cards in hand, there’s a&nbsp;
good chance it’s packing a lot of removal. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.400
deck has a big land count, there are all sorts of&nbsp;
moderately scary things you need to worry about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.840
but the odds of the spookiest outcomes are&nbsp;
lessened the fewer cards in hand the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.240
has. It’s a powerful deck against a lot of&nbsp;
conventionally built mid-power decks—I call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.080
it Midrange-Crusher 5000 for a reason—but&nbsp;
the transparency and absence of sudden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.120
wincons means that people tend to worry&nbsp;
about it less than they probably should.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.320
Another transparent deck I have is my Meria&nbsp;
deck, and though that one has more unique tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:09.480
and response plays, it’s transparent because&nbsp;
it’s a Voltron deck—the problem is right there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.320
on the board in front of you. If there’s&nbsp;
a Meria on board with a lot of equipments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.600
I’m doing pretty well. Most of my draws will be&nbsp;
exiles off the top, so you’re seeing most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.720
stuff I’m seeing—the scary stuff is all visible.&nbsp;
There might be some protection spells in my hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.520
but the deck devotes a full 10% of its space to&nbsp;
protection, so that much shouldn’t be a shock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:31.920
Meria also has the advantage over Glissa that&nbsp;
it’s low-to-the-ground—my deck can create a value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.000
engine with its commander to exile cards, but&nbsp;
that value is more small artifacts and creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.440
and the deck doesn’t do much if the artifact&nbsp;
count is low or the commander gets removed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.640
Even with a high artifact count, the deck still&nbsp;
only hits one player a turn and gains maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.800
four cards worth of value. My $25 Rakdos&nbsp;
discard deck is even lower to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.080
only incrementally drawing cards&nbsp;
with a handful of scrappy creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.080
and honestly that’s the deck’s only saving grace.&nbsp;
It deletes people’s hands and makes them upset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.680
but the rest of the deck is just grindy creatures&nbsp;
and a commander to clear the way for my small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.560
group of 2/2s and 3/3s to attack. People will&nbsp;
sometimes attack it out of spite for a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.480
but the non-discard portion of the deck&nbsp;
is so utterly fair-magic that people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:19.000
usually go “oh, you’re just doing that now.&nbsp;
Huh.” And won’t worry about it too much.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.800
All three of these decks pair well with a&nbsp;
style of table engagement I like to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.160
fight-the-table ideology—they can happily admit&nbsp;
to exactly what they plan to do, they can do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.240
fairly consistently, and they are prepared for&nbsp;
the consequences if they become an enemy. Me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.280
Mr. Hans originally coined this phrase to describe&nbsp;
the optimal playstyle for mixed power level games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.880
or alternatively games where one player gets&nbsp;
a decisive advantage early. If you’re playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.880
more powerfully than other people, you could&nbsp;
play close to the chest before popping off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.120
with a power-turn and deleting the table, but&nbsp;
that’s not really fun for anybody. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.960
give your opponents the information they need to&nbsp;
know and be open about the general shape of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:00.720
your deck is doing, specifically how it wins&nbsp;
and what cards you’re playing are doing there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.960
and suddenly you’ve got a dynamic game&nbsp;
where your opponents are strategizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:08.360
about how to keep you in check while&nbsp;
managing their own individual plans.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.600
Any deck can partake in this ideology in&nbsp;
a game playing against lower power decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.560
and I encourage giving it a try, but as&nbsp;
a method it also works as a more general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.880
style of deckbuilding and play. Now, I can&nbsp;
already hear some of you say “but Alex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.720
why would I want to be the enemy? I don’t&nbsp;
want my life total to be yeeted into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:29.320
sun by a bunch of quivering nerds who I’ve been&nbsp;
smugly informing of their impending demise at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:34.720
the hands of my deck.” Well first, if you are&nbsp;
in a dominant position, doing literally that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:39.400
can lead to some really fun gameplay as long as&nbsp;
you’re not an ass about it. But more broadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.760
this method can actually save your deck&nbsp;
from some hate, and let’s talk about that.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.480
This might seem odd for hate-dodging, but step&nbsp;
one of fight-the-table ideology is that you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.520
pose some sort of clear threat to the table with&nbsp;
a good degree of consistency. This could be early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:59.040
damage, big midgame beats, boardwipes, stax, and&nbsp;
so forth. This will mostly come down to building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.320
a deck that’s focused and isn’t filled with&nbsp;
unnecessary tech pieces or creatures out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:08.080
sync with the gameplan, and you can accomplish&nbsp;
this step on a shockingly low budget. The point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:12.840
of this step is that what you’re doing needs&nbsp;
to be visible. To go back to an earlier point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.720
I’ve seen hyper-techy midrange decks which fill&nbsp;
their board with unusual permanents with no clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.440
sense of how they’ll be used. If you do this,&nbsp;
people might feel a looming sense of threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.720
but with no obvious sense of what to worry about&nbsp;
this might simply result in them attacking you. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.760
contrast, if the threat is visible, people&nbsp;
can make their own educated calculations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:35.800
and that calculation might just be “eh,&nbsp;
it’ll probably be fine.” Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:40.400
consistency is important because that will be&nbsp;
baked into people’s calculations as well. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.320
we take the inconsistent, explosive deck I was&nbsp;
talking about earlier and average out the power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:50.000
it might very well be lower than a deck engaging&nbsp;
in fight-the-table ideology. But people aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.680
gonna be thinking about averages, they’re gonna be&nbsp;
thinking about the fact that you swung for 90 at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:59.120
them on turn 5 last game, and that’s a lot scarier&nbsp;
than a deck that consistently swings for 15.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.720
Step two of fight-the-table ideology is to be open&nbsp;
about the threat your deck poses to the table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:09.880
This doesn’t mean you need to overhype yourself&nbsp;
or give people constant updates on your draws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:14.960
but be straightforward with people. Verbally, this&nbsp;
could mean saying, “yeah my deck generates a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:19.680
of artifact tokens, which I can use to accomplish&nbsp;
various effects. I don’t have very many right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.800
so I’ll need to get a lot more and have an&nbsp;
outlet for them before I can do anything too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:27.600
scary.” In terms of play, being open can&nbsp;
just mean having a thing you’re clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.760
doing. Don’t let your opponents imagine what&nbsp;
you’re doing with all these fishy plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:39.280
have a goal and execute clear steps along the way.&nbsp;
One easy example for this is hardcore ramp decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.320
which work well because everybody knows they’re&nbsp;
scary at a lot of mana, but there’s no singular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.880
land drop that signals the deck being a problem.&nbsp;
It evokes more of a gentle gradient of concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:54.240
from your opponents rather than the black and&nbsp;
white concern of a random spooky permanent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.240
and this can lead to the deck flying under&nbsp;
the radar. This is doubly true if you’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:02.600
running a singular win card like Torment of&nbsp;
Hailfire, but rather a win involving multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.400
cards used in a complex way, since that&nbsp;
can kinda just blend together in people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:11.640
minds as “yeah the deck does big things when it&nbsp;
gets a lot of mana,” which is conceptually less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:16.520
scary than a one shot. This sort of ramp&nbsp;
deck is a great example of transparency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:22.160
being unexpectedly effective at avoiding&nbsp;
attacks compared to more opaque playstyles.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:26.040
I’ve laid out the basic framework, but&nbsp;
a natural question that might arise is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.720
“can my deck be played this way?” I do my best&nbsp;
to play decks that are relatively consistent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.240
so a lot of mine do end up following this mold,&nbsp;
but even in my case some decks will be trickier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:40.040
to do this with than others. Decks with combos&nbsp;
are generally more difficult to do this with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:44.680
as they’re naturally less transparent due to the&nbsp;
level of importance they place on cards in hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:50.000
which is by definition a hidden zone. For this&nbsp;
reason and others, sometimes a deck isn’t a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.320
great candidate for this kind of transparency&nbsp;
treatment. However, I think that a good exercise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:01.120
for every deck is thinking about what it would&nbsp;
have to do to become a good candidate for this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.840
What happens if you remind your opponents of&nbsp;
the strongest wincon of your deck, along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:09.160
the conditions required to get there, right at&nbsp;
the start of every game? What happens if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:14.520
opponents treat that wincon you mention seriously?&nbsp;
If your deck is sometimes a slow midrange deck and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:20.040
other times an explosive combo list, well, that&nbsp;
might be something to interrogate a bit more.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:23.600
I have a couple of other things to say about&nbsp;
“playing like a nerd” and other forms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:28.160
pseudo-politics, but I couldn’t find a nice&nbsp;
place to fit them into this video. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.800
I posted them in a three minute long unlisted&nbsp;
video. I’ve left a link in the description to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:35.680
that video and it’ll probably stay there for a&nbsp;
little while, but at some point I’m gonna edit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:40.640
it out. You’ll be able to find that link on my&nbsp;
patreon after that, and there will likely be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:45.200
endnotes like that posted there in the future,&nbsp;
along with occasional full-length bonus videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:50.400
